// The aerial perspective ON CLOUDS: how the air between the camera and a cloud is composed with the
// cloud's own radiance. Dual-compile dialect (Common/CloudNoise.glslh states the rules); driven as C++
// by Desert/Tests/Engine/SkyScattering.
//
// TWO PASSES STATE THIS, and before this header they stated it twice in their own words:
// Programs/Clouds/CloudRaymarch.shader FETCHES the atmosphere from the froxel volume the sky filled,
// Programs/Compute/BakeProceduralSky.shader INTEGRATES the same air itself because the panorama bake has
// no camera frustum to fetch from. Where the air comes from is genuinely different; what is done with it
// once it arrives is not, and that half is here so the two cannot drift. (The screen pass and the bake
// disagreeing about a cloud term is not hypothetical in this engine — it is why the bake owed a sun
// multiply for a whole programme.)
//
// THE COMPOSITION, and why it is the shape it is. The march hands back a premultiplied pair: `luminance`
// is the light the cloud scattered toward the eye and `cloudTransmittance` is what survives THROUGH it,
// so the frame composites the cloud as `scene = luminance + scene * cloudTransmittance`
// (Programs/Clouds/CloudComposite.shader). The sky pixel that `scene` holds is the FULL integral of the
// same medium along the same ray, and the aerial-perspective pair (`apInScatter`, `apTransmittance`) is
// that same integral truncated at the cloud. Those two facts fix the composition completely:
//
//     sky            = apInScatter + apTransmittance * skyBeyondTheCloud
//     the true pixel = apInScatter + apTransmittance * ( luminance + cloudTransmittance * skyBeyond )
//
// and eliminating skyBeyond between them leaves exactly
//
//     luminance' = apInScatter * ( 1 - cloudTransmittance ) + apTransmittance * luminance
//
// which is what CloudApplyAerialPerspective computes, and is Unreal's line verbatim
// (VolumetricCloud.usf: `Luminance = AerialPerspective.rgb * CloudMeanCoverage + AerialPerspective.a *
// Luminance`). The consequence worth stating, because it is the relation the tests assert: after this the
// cloud's contrast against the sky beside it is multiplied by `apTransmittance` and by NOTHING else, so a
// cloud the atmosphere has erased is the sky, rather than being dark, bright or the wrong hue.
//
// `apTransmittance` is a SCALAR — the mean of the three channels — and that is deliberate on both sides:
// the froxel volume has one lane left after the in-scatter, and Unreal collapses it the same way for the
// same reason ("We use gray scale transmittance to match the rendering when applying the AerialPerspective
// texture", SkyAtmosphere.usf). What it costs is the reddening of the cloud's OWN light at long range; the
// light the air ADDS keeps its full spectrum, and that is the term that dominates.

#ifndef CLOUD_AERIAL_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_AERIAL_GLSLH

// The one distance the whole term is evaluated at: the transmittance-weighted mean of the marched
// samples, i.e. where the cloud the eye can actually see is, not where the ray entered the shell. The
// weights are recorded BEFORE each sample attenuates the ray, so they measure what a sample was able to
// contribute. @p viewDistanceScale is the atmosphere's own art-direction dial
// (SkyAtmosphereComponent::AerialPerspectiveViewDistanceScale), applied on the read side by every
// consumer of the volume because the volume itself is filled without it.
//
// Callers must have found at least one sample; with no weight there is no cloud to haze and the term is
// skipped entirely rather than evaluated at a fabricated distance.
float CloudAerialMeanDistanceKm( float weightedDistanceKm, float weightSum, float viewDistanceScale )
{
    return ( weightedDistanceKm / max( weightSum, 1e-8f ) ) * max( viewDistanceScale, 0.0f );
}

// How much of the atmosphere to apply, in [0,1]. Full is the physical answer and both engines' default;
// a fade distance of zero means "in full, immediately", which is the ramp degenerating rather than a
// special case. Unreal computes the same ramp as `saturate( ( distance - start ) * fadeInverse )`.
//
// This is an ART-DIRECTION dial and it can only ever REMOVE atmosphere, never add it: at less than 1 the
// cloud is being shown through less air than the sky beside it is, which is a deliberate lie and looks
// like one when it is pushed far. BELOW the start distance the atmosphere is not weakened, it is absent —
// which is the part that surprises, because the name says "start" and reads as "the haze begins here".
//
// ONE DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM UNREAL, stated because a setting ported between the two will not mean
// the same thing. Unreal's start distance ALSO shifts the volume lookup (SkyAtmosphereCommon.ush:
// `tDepth = max( 0, length - StartDepth )`), so its cloud reads the froxel at a shorter distance than it
// actually is, and it therefore samples air the sky beside it is not looking through. We read the volume
// at the cloud's true distance and ramp only the amount, which is what keeps the relation the whole term
// is for: cloud and sky at one distance see one atmosphere. At the default of 0 the two engines are
// identical; above it ours applies more haze at the same setting, and ours is the one that stays honest.
float CloudAerialAmount( float meanDistanceKm, float startDistanceKm, float fadeDistanceKm )
{
    if ( fadeDistanceKm <= 0.0f )
        return 1.0f;
    return clamp( ( meanDistanceKm - startDistanceKm ) / fadeDistanceKm, 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

// The composition itself. @p luminance and @p cloudTransmittance are the march's premultiplied pair;
// @p apInScatter and @p apTransmittance are the atmosphere between the camera and the cloud.
//
// The @p amount lerp is applied to the RESULT rather than to the atmosphere sample, and the two are the
// same thing: fading the sample toward "no atmosphere" — Unreal's `AP.rgb *= w; AP.a = 1 - w * (1 - AP.a)`
// — expands to exactly this mix, which the tests pin so the cheaper form here stays honest.
vec3 CloudApplyAerialPerspective( vec3 luminance, float cloudTransmittance, vec3 apInScatter,
                                  float apTransmittance, float amount )
{
    float cloudCoverage = 1.0f - clamp( cloudTransmittance, 0.0f, 1.0f );
    vec3  hazed         = apInScatter * cloudCoverage + apTransmittance * luminance;
    return mix( luminance, hazed, clamp( amount, 0.0f, 1.0f ) );
}

#endif // CLOUD_AERIAL_GLSLH
